.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:43:Contract: toolchain-registry-v1 maps each driver to one primary toolchain plus companions over the closed id set go/rust/swift/kotlin/jdk; go entry complete, rust/swift/kotlin/jdk reserved with a fixed obligation list their own driver decisions must fill on a qualified host. toolchain-requirement-v1 is exactly {id, version} with exact/at_least/range over a canonical major.minor.patch triple; combination is interval intersection (associative, commutative, source-order-independent) and an empty intersection is rejected before the host is probed. Prereleases satisfy nothing and cannot appear in a requirement, because no cross-language prerelease order exists. Resolution admits only manager-bundled or trusted operator config; the forbidden-origin and package-influence lists are exhaustive. Two-stage preflight: Stage A (availability/version/platform) right after manifest parse, before external acquisition, cache lookup and persistent mutation; Stage B (go.mod / Cargo.toml + rust-toolchain.toml / swift-tools-version / selected Kotlin field) after local validation or exact external acquisition and audit, before any cache candidate read or compiler child. Metadata is an assertion, never a selector. Eleven stable build_toolchain_* codes carry a guidance_id only; toolchain-guidance-catalog-v1 is manager-owned, versioned, total (release-gate enforced), primary-source-only, text+URL never a command. No auto-install in v1, named explicitly for rustup/swiftly/sdkman/asdf/mise/Homebrew/winget/Gradle wrapper/GOTOOLCHAIN.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:102:(1) Go classifier exhaustive. New reference 4.2.1-4.2.4. One shared goVersionLiteral grammar yielding a base triple plus prerelease flag. The go directive has 4 total classes and NO forbidden class - a value with a path separator is unclassifiable, not package influence, stated explicitly. The toolchain directive has 7 total ordered classes: path-bearing and custom-distribution (go1.23.4-bigcorp) are forbidden, default is permitted and never honored, canonical release and prerelease names compare by base triple, catch-all is mismatch; a repeated directive is the catch-all. Base-triple canonicalization is exact rather than approximate: Go orders 1.21 < 1.21rc1 < 1.21.0, and the two orders can differ only for a comparand strictly between a language version and its release, every such value being a prerelease that Stage A already rejected. go.work is deliberately not a metadata source because protocol/core.md 4.2 fixes GOWORK=off. Grammar taken from on-host primary sources (modfile ToolchainRE/GoVersionRE, gover.FromToolchain), not from memory. Supporting rule: new 3.1.1 value classifiers for a field whose value space spans dispositions - ordered classes, forbidden first, mandatory catch-all.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:146:Rework cycle 4 — both cycle-4 blocking findings closed. (1) The go-directive classifier is no longer defined by modfile.GoVersionRE alone. Section 4.2.1 now names the two upstream layers Go composes — modfile shape (GoVersionRE/ToolchainRE) and gover semantics (Parse/FromToolchain) — and defines admitted values as their conjunction at both positions. The layers are incomparable in both directions, so neither alone is the language. go 1.23.4rc1, 1.24.0alpha1 and 1.21.3beta2 are now class 4 metadata_mismatch at Stage B step 5, before cache lookup and before a compiler child; vector 122 replaced, 122a-122d and 124a added, 118 restated as the opposite half of the boundary. Grammars renamed goModVersionLiteral->goModVersionShape and goToolchainVersion->goSemanticVersion. Stated explicitly why this is a classifier case and not the file-shape gate: the file parses and the field extracts, so what rejects it is version semantics, not file syntax; section 5 firing-site list now carries that split. (2) Vector 126 replaced. New section 4.2.1.1 states P1 (C subset of Upstream, no widening) and P2 (Upstream minus F subset of C, no narrowing outside the security partition), giving C = Upstream \ F. C = Upstream is recorded as unsatisfiable rather than strict, because upstream accepts go1.21.0-custom and this contract forbids it; F is neither a subset of nor disjoint from Upstream (go1.23/../evil is forbidden and upstream-rejected), so only C is pinned. F is empty for the go directive, where P2 collapses to equality. Vectors 126/126a-126d cover both properties, the subtraction witness, the F-not-subset witness, and empty F. (3) New section 4.2.1.2 makes the executable probe a normative obligation, implemented and run in .temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/probe: 13+13 values on Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, exit 0, zero divergences, measuring layers independently via go mod edit -json, go mod tidy, and offline GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path. Every upstream fact this cycle was read from this host GOROOT and vendored x/mod or measured by the probe; nothing carried forward from a prior cycle assertion. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly three files; conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical. Gates standalone with real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas, 422 vector files), unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0 no output, probe gofmt/vet/run 0. Clean probe 02e4ebc: regenerate-check 0 twice, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 0. Three expected-red probes reported as failing because they are: injected broken link -> validate exit 1; cycle-3 classification restored -> probe exit 1 with P1 VIOLATED by 1.23.4rc1; C = Upstream forced -> probe exit 1 with security partition subtracts MISMATCH. All reverted, post-revert green. Nothing staged, committed, published, or pinned; no platform claim for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:166:Cases. New go-directive cases 1.99.0, 1.26.0, 1.99rc1; inventory 13+13 -> 16+13, 58 measured cases across Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, zero divergences and zero isolated-versus-command disagreements. Reference gains 122e/122f/122g (future release and future prerelease fail on the comparison with a derived assertion, not on the grammar; a future release below the host stays permitted) and 127a/127b/127c (isolation, agreement, controls). Decision gains four rejected alternatives and an isolated-measurement obligation for TASK-260728-2jaw7h and for the reserved rust/swift/kotlin/jdk entries. Two probe bugs also fixed: an inherited GOROOT collapsed a two-toolchain run into one, and toolchainVersion did not pin GOTOOLCHAIN=local.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:194:Fix, three parts. (1) Command narrowed: the toolchain-position corroborating command is `go version` under GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path, whose entire failure surface is toolchain.Select, so exit 0 measures acceptance instead of reporting that nothing else went wrong. Read from this host GOROOT: go version is not one of the commands Select short-circuits (only go env GOTOOLCHAIN / -w GOTOOLCHAIN= / GOMOD / GOWORK are), and measurement confirms it - go env GOMOD exits 0 for all 13 values including go2.0.0, go version reproduces every selection outcome. (2) Fourth state cmdUnknown added: not a verdict, always fails, and the agreement check is skipped for that row rather than being fed a fabricated one. (3) Recognition closed and structural, matched per diagnostic line after stripping cmd/go own `go: ` prefix, every version-bearing form required to echo the exact value under test in upstream %q. toolchain: exit 0 | cannot find "v" in PATH -> accepted; invalid toolchain "v" in <file> | invalid GOTOOLCHAIN "v" -> rejected; else unknown. go: exit 0 | TooNew echoing v -> accepted/too-new; invalid go version 'v': | the fixed missing-go-root-module abort -> rejected; else unknown.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:196:Also closed the go-directive classifier, beyond the directive: its fall-through named rejection, which launders an unrelated failure just as effectively for any value the isolated measurement already rejects, where the two would again agree for the wrong reason. Requiring the echoed name is load-bearing beyond tidiness: Select renders two further invalid GOTOOLCHAIN %q messages that quote the ENVIRONMENT value (local+path), never the go.mod name; those now fall to unknown, correctly.
