.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1ljev5/TASK-260720-1ljev5_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-f503ea.log:6795:- FINDING: `go 1.99.0` is accepted by `GoVersionRE` and `gover.Parse`; on both Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, `go mod edit -json` exits 0 while `GOTOOLCHAIN=local go mod tidy` exits 1 only because the running host is too old (`go.mod requires go >= 1.99.0`). The probe would therefore mark a valid compared value as upstream-rejected and falsely fail P1.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1ljev5/TASK-260720-1ljev5_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-f503ea.log:6836:- FINDING: the `toolchain` directive's semantic layer can be probed **offline** with `GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path` — a representable name yields `cannot find "X" in PATH`, an unrepresentable one yields `invalid toolchain "X" in go.mod` before any lookup. No download, no installed toolchain needed.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1ljev5/TASK-260720-1ljev5_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-f503ea.log:6838:- DECISION: alignment with an ecosystem grammar cannot be stated as set equality when a security partition subtracts from it. Upstream accepts `go1.21.0-custom`; Curator forbids custom-distribution names. Split into P1 `C ⊆ Upstream` (no widening) and P2 `Upstream \ F ⊆ C` (no narrowing outside the forbidden partition), giving `C = Upstream \ F`. `F` is neither a subset of nor disjoint from `Upstream`, so only `C` is pinned.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1ljev5/TASK-260720-1ljev5_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-f503ea.log:6990:- DECISION: a custom Go toolchain name (`go1.23.4-bigcorp`) is `package_influence_forbidden`, not a metadata mismatch. A mismatch asserts "this claim is false about the resolved toolchain", which invites a later implementation to verify the claim by consulting the named distribution — reopening the selection path the contract exists to close. `toolchain default` is the opposite case: permitted and never honored, because it asserts exactly Curator's own unconditional behavior.
