ion: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-092e80, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-092e80)\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-092e80, pid=29455, exit=0)\nREWORK CYCLE 2 directive 2026-07-28: queued for the first free Claude Opus 5 producer slot; continue only in the existing task-owned curator-spec worktree and preserve the accepted predecessor read-only. Close all four cycle-2 blockers from TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-2.md: (1) make the complete Go metadata classifier exhaustive over every officially admitted ToolchainName class, including toolchain default, canonical release names, and custom/non-canonical names, with deterministic outcomes and focused vectors; (2) partition or explicitly order overlapping wire invalid-vs-package-influence and Stage-A unavailable-vs-untrusted triggers, with overlap vectors; (3) define a representable typed diagnostic payload union/optionality for invalid and empty-intersection requirements, including retained validated source fragments and payload-shape vectors; (4) define allowed guidance coverage modes, exact-OS plus any fallback behavior, and append-only/cross-version retirement transitions, with hybrid and transition vectors. Preserve the twelve-code totality, tested-family gate, revisioned IDs, source-metadata disposition split, cache ordering, all rc.5 bytes, and the previous five accepted fixes. No schema/release/pin/commit/staging change; rerun deterministic and unchanged-rc.5 gates, attach a new rework artifact, and hand off to a new independent reviewer cycle.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-1ccd8b, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-1ccd8b)\nRework cycle 2 complete: all four cycle-2 blocking findings closed in the same task worktree /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/curator-spec-worktree. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly decisions/0007-compiled-build-toolchain-preflight.md, docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md and one CHANGELOG bullet. Nothing staged, committed, published, pinned; no platform validation claimed. Predecessor worktree preserved read-only.\n\n(1) Go classifier exhaustive. New reference 4.2.1-4.2.4. One shared goVersionLiteral grammar yielding a base triple plus prerelease flag. The go directive has 4 total classes and NO forbidden class - a value with a path separator is unclassifiable, not package influence, stated explicitly. The toolchain directive has 7 total ordered classes: path-bearing and custom-distribution (go1.23.4-bigcorp) are forbidden, default is permitted and never honored, canonical release and prerelease names compare by base triple, catch-all is mismatch; a repeated directive is the catch-all. Base-triple canonicalization is exact rather than approximate: Go orders 1.21 \u003c 1.21rc1 \u003c 1.21.0, and the two orders can differ only for a comparand strictly between a language version and its release, every such value being a prerelease that Stage A already rejected. go.work is deliberately not a metadata source because protocol/core.md 4.2 fixes GOWORK=off. Grammar taken from on-host primary sources (modfile ToolchainRE/GoVersionRE, gover.FromToolchain), not from memory. Supporting rule: new 3.1.1 value classifiers for a field whose value space spans dispositions - ordered classes, forbidden first, mandatory catch-all.\n\n(2) Overlapping triggers partitioned, not ordered. Wire surface: unknown key is the existing unknown-field schema rejection, malformed value is build_toolchain_requirement_invalid, so package_influence_forbidden never fires on the wire and is a Stage B code only. Rationale: classifying a malformed literal as smuggled influence requires inferring intent from a byte string and two managers would infer differently. The field-addition prohibition moves to a release gate over published schema property names, owned by TASK-260728-2jaw7h, because a field that does not exist produces no value to diagnose. Stage A step 2 splits into 2a declaration / 2b origin trust / 2c shape: unavailable means nothing was declared and is produced only by 2a; untrusted means something was declared and is unusable, including a declared root that does not exist. The cut is declaration presence, not severity, and it matches the existing guidance classes (host vs configuration).\n\n(3) Payload representable. New 5.1: a discriminated union whose shape is derived, not chosen - a code carries exactly the values established where it fires, and the stage steps are totally ordered, so shape is a function of the code. effective_requirement absent in exactly two codes, resolved_version and prerelease absent in exactly five. requirement_invalid carries source_ref plus a closed violation token and must not echo the offending value (no unvalidated package byte in a payload); requirement_unsatisfiable carries the individually validated fragments plus the max-lower and min-upper bounds with every source achieving each in fixed order, so the payload is as source-order-independent as the intersection.\n\n(4) Catalog unambiguous. Coverage is defined by the selection function: any, per_os and hybrid are all valid, and the gate checks resolution (every registry OS resolves to exactly one active entry) plus reachability (an exact entry for an OS outside the registry, or an any entry shadowed everywhere, fails). New 6.2.1 states append-only against the catalog version: a published version is immutable in whole, every change is an N to N+1 transition, the entry set only grows, active and superseded_by are one-way monotone, and four transitions are admissible including retire-without-successor only for a tuple the gate no longer requires. Exactly-one-active-per-tuple relaxes to at-most-one.\n\nVector inventory 58 -\u003e 95 cases: Go classifier positives 9/10/12/13/14 and negatives 45-54 including class boundary 47 and value-class precedence 54; overlap vectors 24-28 and 33-34; payload-shape vectors 65-71; catalog coverage 79-84 and version transitions 85-90. Decision 0007 gained eight rejected alternatives.\n\nGates, standalone, real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files), python unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0, generate-vectors 0. Regenerated conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical to the accepted predecessor. Clean git-init probe clean-probe-c2 at 773147f: regenerate-check x2 = 0, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 = 0. Two negative probes against the reworked files expected-red and red: retired descriptor stem rejected by validate (exit 1), broken local link rejected by validate (exit 1); both reverted, final validate 0 and both files byte-identical to their pre-probe copies. make regenerate-check still cannot pass inside the task worktree because it diffs against 57c1f56 with the rc.5 candidate uncommitted - determinism proven by predecessor byte-comparison and the clean probe. Python gates use the existing validation-venv because ambient python3 lacks jsonschema.\n\nReviewer focus: is the wire partition airtight in both directions; does 2a-2c leave any resolution failure unclassified; are the seven toolchain classes exhaustive over upstream grammar and is custom-distribution-as-package-influence right versus mismatch; does the base-triple equivalence hold for every Stage B comparison including short literals Curator admits but Go rejects; is payload shape a function of the code for all twelve and does no-echo leave any diagnostic under-informative; do resolution plus reachability fully replace the coverage-mode question and do the four version transitions cover every real catalog edit.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-1ccd8b, pid=42374, exit=0)\nREVIEW CYCLE 3 directive 2026-07-28: independently verify the four cycle-2 findings on the final rework, not merely its green gates. Challenge the total Go metadata classifier against official ToolchainName grammar, including default, canonical releases/prereleases, custom distributions, path-bearing and unclassifiable names; confirm deterministic outcomes and no package selector is honored. Verify wire invalid-vs-Stage-B package-influence and Stage-A unavailable-vs-untrusted are disjoint by input, not overlapping by outcome. Verify diagnostic payload shapes are representable, deterministic, retain only validated fragments, and never echo unvalidated package bytes. Verify any/per_os/hybrid guidance coverage resolves every supported OS with no unreachable active entry and catalog N-to-N+1 retirement is monotone while published versions stay immutable. Confirm the 95-case normative inventory is complete for this design task, prior fixes remain intact, and all rc.5/schema/vector/release bytes remain unchanged. Re-run focused adversarial probes and deterministic/release gates; no edits, staging, commit, publication, pins or platform claims; attach explicit verdict and route status.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-0e6d1a, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-0e6d1a)\nReviewer cycle 3 verdict: changes requested. Evidence attached as TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-3.md. Route to analysis for four contract corrections: disjoint Stage-A declaration/origin triggers; an explicit Stage-B rejection when a late descriptor requirement excludes the resolved host; deterministic platform applicability before OS-specific resolution/probing; and a complete pre-compiler outcome for Go-invalid directive values/file shapes. All submitted non-mutating gates pass and frozen rc.5 bytes remain unchanged.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-0e6d1a, pid=63458, exit=0)\nREWORK CYCLE 3 directive 2026-07-28: queued for the first free Claude Opus 5 producer slot; continue in the existing task-owned worktree and close exactly the four blockers in TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-3.md. (1) Make Stage-A declaration/origin partition executable: declaration-presence is tested without judging origin; absent declaration alone yields unavailable, declared forbidden/untrusted origin proceeds deterministically to untrusted, with exact overlap vectors. (2) After an external descriptor joins at Stage B, recompute the intersection and reject both empty late intersection and non-empty requirement that excludes the already resolved host before cache/compiler work; define stable code, stage and payload semantics plus vectors. (3) Check registry host OS/architecture applicability before any platform-specific relpath resolution, executable-shape check or probe construction, with deterministic platform_unsupported precedence and vectors. (4) Align Go metadata parsing with official validated file grammar and provide complete typed pre-compiler outcomes for short/zero-major values, repeated go/toolchain directives and malformed file shapes; no invalid source reaches compiler work. Preserve the payload/catalog fixes, tested-family gate, classifier security properties, prior review closures and all rc.5 bytes; update the normative vector inventory, rerun deterministic/release gates, attach rework evidence and hand off to a fresh reviewer.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-2ce7ec, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-2ce7ec)\nRework cycle 3 ready for review. Closes all four cycle-3 blockers. (1) Declaration/origin triggers made disjoint by input: new declaration-channel model in section 3 (operator_config then bundled; PATH is not a channel), Stage A sub-steps 3a channel map / 3b entry value+holding state / 3c filesystem object; PATH-installed-but-undeclared host is unavailable from 3a, vectored. (2) Stage B is now an ordered five-step algorithm: re-compute interval, re-evaluate resolved version against it, file-shape gate, forbidden, compared. incompatible and requirement_unsatisfiable each get one firing site per interval; compatibility explicitly not re-evaluated. (3) Host-pair applicability moved to Stage A step 2 ahead of relpath resolution and probing, backed by a new registry totality rule for primary_relpath/probe over platforms plus a release-gate reachability check; native-target half stays at step 6; new REQUIRED check discriminant selects the payload branch. (4) Go grammars pinned per position to upstream verbatim (modfile.GoVersionRE, modfile.ToolchainRE, gover.Parse) with no widening either way, so go 1 / go 0 / go 1.023 get a typed metadata_mismatch before compiler work; repeated go and toolchain directives move to the new file-shape gate. Payload union re-keyed on firing site (code, stage, discriminant) with stage as a REQUIRED common member. Vector inventory 95 to 126. Delta versus accepted predecessor is still exactly decision 0007, the reference guide, and one CHANGELOG bullet; conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical (diff exit 0). Gates, each standalone with real exit codes: validate.py 0 (42 schemas / 422 vectors), unittest 0 (29), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt -l tools 0 with no output, git diff --check 0 with no output; clean-probe 41e8250 regenerate-check 0 and release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 0.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-2ce7ec, pid=73782, exit=0)\nREVIEW CYCLE 4 directive 2026-07-28: independently verify all four cycle-3 blockers are actually closed in the final three-file delta. (1) Stage-A declaration presence reads only operator_config/bundled channel map; PATH-only with no declaration is unavailable, while a declared forbidden value/state is untrusted, with disjoint origin/shape triggers. (2) Stage B recomputes the effective interval and rejects both empty intersection and a non-empty interval excluding the already resolved host before cache/compiler, with deterministic site-keyed diagnostics. (3) host platform applicability precedes every OS-specific relpath/probe/shape access and platform_unsupported payload distinguishes host_pair from native_target. (4) Go metadata uses exact upstream per-position grammars and typed pre-compiler outcomes for short/zero-major, repeated go/toolchain directives and malformed files. Challenge algorithm ordering and payload-union closure, rerun all 126 vector cases and Python/Go/format/diff gates, deterministic regeneration and rc.5 release/byte identity. Confirm only CHANGELOG, decision 0007 and toolchain reference differ; no edits/staging/commit/publication/pins/platform claims. Attach explicit ACCEPTED or CHANGES REQUESTED verdict and route status.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-8a585f, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-8a585f)\nReviewer cycle 4 verdict: changes requested and routed to analysis. Evidence: TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-4.md. Blocking rework: close the GoVersionRE versus gover.Parse semantic gap exposed by go 1.23.4rc1, and replace the impossible vector-126 equality with separate grammar-completeness and security-forbidden partition properties.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-8a585f, pid=90709, exit=0)\nREWORK CYCLE 4 directive 2026-07-28: continue in the existing three-file task worktree and close only both blockers in TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-4.md. (1) Define go-directive admission over the complete upstream pipeline: lexical modfile.GoVersionRE plus semantic cmd/go/internal/gover.Parse (or an exactly sourced equivalent), so patch-prerelease forms such as go 1.23.4rc1 receive a stable typed pre-compiler metadata outcome and cannot reach Go compiler/package work. Replace vector 122 and add an executable adversarial probe reproducing the regex-pass/semantic-reject boundary against available upstream Go versions. (2) Correct vector 126: do not claim compared-set equality with all upstream-accepted toolchain names when custom distributions are intentionally security-forbidden; state separate grammar completeness after the forbidden partition and selector-exclusion/no-widening invariants. Preserve all prior ordering/payload fixes, exact three-file delta, 12 diagnostics/guidance contract, rc.5 bytes and no staging/commit/publish/pin/platform claim. Rerun 126+ updated vectors, Python/Go/format/diff, clean deterministic regeneration and rc.5 release gates; attach rework-04 evidence and hand off to independent review cycle 5.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-65ee58, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-65ee58)\nRework cycle 4 — both cycle-4 blocking findings closed. (1) The go-directive classifier is no longer defined by modfile.GoVersionRE alone. Section 4.2.1 now names the two upstream layers Go composes — modfile shape (GoVersionRE/ToolchainRE) and gover semantics (Parse/FromToolchain) — and defines admitted values as their conjunction at both positions. The layers are incomparable in both directions, so neither alone is the language. go 1.23.4rc1, 1.24.0alpha1 and 1.21.3beta2 are now class 4 metadata_mismatch at Stage B step 5, before cache lookup and before a compiler child; vector 122 replaced, 122a-122d and 124a added, 118 restated as the opposite half of the boundary. Grammars renamed goModVersionLiteral-\u003egoModVersionShape and goToolchainVersion-\u003egoSemanticVersion. Stated explicitly why this is a classifier case and not the file-shape gate: the file parses and the field extracts, so what rejects it is version semantics, not file syntax; section 5 firing-site list now carries that split. (2) Vector 126 replaced. New section 4.2.1.1 states P1 (C subset of Upstream, no widening) and P2 (Upstream minus F subset of C, no narrowing outside the security partition), giving C = Upstream \\ F. C = Upstream is recorded as unsatisfiable rather than strict, because upstream accepts go1.21.0-custom and this contract forbids it; F is neither a subset of nor disjoint from Upstream (go1.23/../evil is forbidden and upstream-rejected), so only C is pinned. F is empty for the go directive, where P2 collapses to equality. Vectors 126/126a-126d cover both properties, the subtraction witness, the F-not-subset witness, and empty F. (3) New section 4.2.1.2 makes the executable probe a normative obligation, implemented and run in .temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/probe: 13+13 values on Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, exit 0, zero divergences, measuring layers independently via go mod edit -json, go mod tidy, and offline GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path. Every upstream fact this cycle was read from this host GOROOT and vendored x/mod or measured by the probe; nothing carried forward from a prior cycle assertion. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly three files; conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical. Gates standalone with real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas, 422 vector files), unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0 no output, probe gofmt/vet/run 0. Clean probe 02e4ebc: regenerate-check 0 twice, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 0. Three expected-red probes reported as failing because they are: injected broken link -\u003e validate exit 1; cycle-3 classification restored -\u003e probe exit 1 with P1 VIOLATED by 1.23.4rc1; C = Upstream forced -\u003e probe exit 1 with security partition subtracts MISMATCH. All reverted, post-revert green. Nothing staged, committed, published, or pinned; no platform claim for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-65ee58, pid=97219, exit=0)\nREVIEW CYCLE 5 directive: independently verify both cycle-4 blockers are closed. For Go directives, prove upstream acceptance is the complete conjunction of parser shape and semantic representability, and challenge whether any third layer can reject a value after both measured layers accept; reproduce 1.23.4rc1, 1.24.0alpha1, 1.21.3beta2 and valid 1.23rc1 boundaries on both available Go toolchains. For toolchain directives, verify P1 C subset Upstream and P2 Upstream minus F subset C are satisfiable and together pin C exactly, while the forbidden security partition remains independently closed and cannot become a selector/install/path escape. Run the executable probe and red regressions, all spec validation/unit/Go/vet/gofmt/diff gates, deterministic regeneration twice, and rc.5 release/byte-identity checks. Confirm the exact three-file scope and no staging/commit/publish/pin/platform claim. Record an independent ACCEPTED or CHANGES REQUESTED verdict; accept only if no compiler-side panic/deferred semantic case remains.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-18b23e, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-18b23e)\nReviewer cycle 5 verdict: changes requested. Evidence attached as TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-5.md and logged in LOGBOOK.md entry 2347. Route to analysis. Blocking issue: the required go mod tidy semantic probe conflates gover representability with the running toolchain TooNew gate; go 1.99.0 is shape-valid and representable but tidy exits 1 on both Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, so the mandated exit-code classifier falsely marks it upstream-rejected and violates its own P1 definition. Isolate gover.Parse or count typed TooNew as representable; add future-version-on-older-host and expected-red regression cases while preserving prior closures and frozen rc.5 bytes.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-18b23e, pid=9292, exit=0)\nREWORK CYCLE 5 directive: close only TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-5.md in the existing exact three-file worktree. Replace exit-code-only go mod tidy semantic classification with an isolated gover.Parse-equivalent measurement, or a rigorously parsed upstream command outcome that counts TooNewError as representable while rejecting invalid-toolchain and patch-prerelease semantic failures/panics. Add a future release case such as go 1.99.0 on both Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, and an expected-red regression proving the old tidy-exit classifier misclassifies it. Keep go 1.23.4rc1/1.24.0alpha1/1.21.3beta2 rejected and 1.23rc1 accepted; preserve P1/P2 and forbidden-selector partition, all prior ordering/payload/guidance fixes, exact three-file scope, frozen rc.5 bytes, no staging/commit/publish/pin/platform claim. Update decision, reference and executable probe obligation consistently; rerun probe, red controls, full validation/unit/Go/vet/gofmt/diff, deterministic regeneration twice and rc.5 release gate; attach rework-05 evidence and hand off for cycle 6 review.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-cedcf7, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-cedcf7)\nRework cycle 5 ready for review. Closes the single cycle-5 blocker. Reproduced first on both toolchains: a module with go 1.99.0 gives go mod edit -json exit 0 and go mod tidy exit 1 with the TooNew message, so the cycle-4 exit-code-only step 2 scored a representable future release as outside Upstream and contradicted its own class-2 classification.\n\nFix. The semantic layer is now stated as representability, a property of the value alone, with upstream evidence read from this host: cmd/go/internal/modload raises *gover.TooNewError only after modfile.Parse returns and gover.Compare(f.Go.Version, gover.Local()) \u003e 0, and internal/gover imports only cmp. The probe lifts internal/gover/gover.go whole and FromToolchain verbatim out of the probed toolchain own GOROOT, builds them with that toolchain, and evaluates representability directly; the real command is kept only as corroboration with its outcome classified into accepted/too-new/rejected. Too-new is recognised structurally - the echoed version must equal the value under test - not by a loose grep. The two measurements must agree wherever the shape layer accepts the value. Contract argument for the split: Curator already reaches the host relation at its own firing site, since a future release is a class-2 comparison above the resolved triple reported with a derived canonical assertion, so folding the host gate into the grammar would classify one fact twice under two payloads and move every representable future release into the unclassifiable class.\n\nCases. New go-directive cases 1.99.0, 1.26.0, 1.99rc1; inventory 13+13 -\u003e 16+13, 58 measured cases across Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, zero divergences and zero isolated-versus-command disagreements. Reference gains 122e/122f/122g (future release and future prerelease fail on the comparison with a derived assertion, not on the grammar; a future release below the host stays permitted) and 127a/127b/127c (isolation, agreement, controls). Decision gains four rejected alternatives and an isolated-measurement obligation for TASK-260728-2jaw7h and for the reserved rust/swift/kotlin/jdk entries. Two probe bugs also fixed: an inherited GOROOT collapsed a two-toolchain run into one, and toolchainVersion did not pin GOTOOLCHAIN=local.\n\nControls. The three superseded classifications are now runnable from the probe binary instead of hand-edited copies, each required to fail.\n\nGates, standalone, real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files), unittest 0 (29), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0 no output, generate-vectors 0, probe gofmt/vet 0, probe -semantic isolated 0. Clean probe 9c752ea: regenerate-check 0 twice, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 0. Regenerated conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical to the accepted predecessor (diff exit 0 both). Reported truthfully as failing: make regenerate-check inside the task worktree exits 1, because it diffs against 57c1f56 with the rc.5 candidate uncommitted; determinism is established by the predecessor byte-comparison and the clean probe instead, as in cycles 1-4. Five expected-red controls reported as failing because they are: -semantic tidy-exit exit 1 with P1 VIOLATED by 1.26.0/1.99.0/1.99rc1 on both toolchains, -red patch-prerelease-compared exit 1 with P1 VIOLATED by 1.23.4rc1/1.24.0alpha1/1.21.3beta2, -red c-equals-upstream exit 1 with the security partition not subtracting, injected broken link validate exit 1, injected retired descriptor stem validate exit 1; both injections reverted, file byte-identical to its pre-probe copy, post-revert validate 0.\n\nDelta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly CHANGELOG.md, decisions/0007 and docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md. Nothing staged, committed, published or pinned; no platform validation claimed for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk. Predecessor worktree preserved read-only at 57c1f56.\n\nReviewer focus: is representability the right definition of the semantic layer given that the class-2 comparison is where the host relation belongs; is lifting the toolchain own gover sources a sound isolation or does it measure a package rather than the command composition; is requiring agreement only where the shape layer accepts too weak; is the structural too-new discriminator robust against an upstream error that satisfies it without representation; is any fourth acceptance layer still unmeasured for the toolchain position.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-cedcf7, pid=15771, exit=0)\nReview cycle 6